1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,130
Can you show me kubectl fundamentals?

2
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:13,849
kubectl ("kube control") is the command-line client of Kubernetes.

3
00:00:13,849 --> 00:00:18,403
Every kubectl command becomes a request to the API server:

4
00:00:18,403 --> 00:00:22,643
list these objects, create this one, show me its logs.

5
00:00:22,643 --> 00:00:29,081
Learning kubectl is learning to ask the cluster questions and to read its answers.

6
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:35,346
Kubernetes has no screen of its own; the API is the only way in.

7
00:00:35,346 --> 00:00:43,469
kubectl is the tool every Kubernetes engineer uses daily, on every cluster, cloud or local.

8
00:00:43,469 --> 00:00:48,290
The same twenty commands cover almost all of the work:

9
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:53,199
looking (get, describe, logs, events), changing (apply,

10
00:00:53,199 --> 00:00:58,198
delete, edit, scale) and debugging (exec, port-forward).

11
00:00:59,933 --> 00:01:02,353
And how does it actually work?

12
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:07,608
The pattern is always kubectl VERB [TYPE] [NAME] [flags]:

13
00:01:07,608 --> 00:01:11,160
kubectl get pod web -n kubectl-lab -o yaml.

14
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:19,581
Every command below comes with its purpose, syntax and the options you will use most.

