49
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:51,831
What do I need to remember?

50
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:56,785
kubectl VERB TYPE NAME -n NS -o FORMAT:

51
00:03:56,785 --> 00:04:04,941
get, describe, logs, exec, apply, delete, explain, port-forward, config.

52
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:11,804
apply -f is declarative ("make it so"); create is imperative ("do this once").

53
00:04:12,367 --> 00:04:21,616
describe + get events explain problems; -o wide/yaml/custom-columns/jsonpath show exactly what you need.

54
00:04:23,367 --> 00:04:26,394
I keep hearing about namespaces.

55
00:04:26,394 --> 00:04:28,191
What is it, really?

56
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:32,729
A namespace is a named folder inside the cluster.

57
00:04:32,729 --> 00:04:36,154
Most objects (Pods, Deployments, Services,

58
00:04:36,154 --> 00:04:43,738
ConfigMaps…) live in exactly one namespace, and their names only have to be unique inside it.

59
00:04:43,738 --> 00:04:50,262
One cluster can hold dev, test and prod side by side, each with its own web Pod.

60
00:04:50,262 --> 00:04:50,914
Analogy:

61
00:04:50,914 --> 00:04:57,683
one office building (the cluster), many companies on different floors (namespaces).

62
00:04:58,233 --> 00:04:59,299
Organization:

63
00:04:59,299 --> 00:05:08,723
a team, an application or an environment gets its own space; kubectl get pods -n dev shows only what belongs there.

64
00:05:08,723 --> 00:05:10,280
Isolation of names:

65
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:13,394
two teams can both call something web.

66
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:16,590
Resource management and access control:

67
00:05:16,590 --> 00:05:25,523
quotas (how much CPU and memory a namespace may use) and permissions (RBAC, lesson 24) are set per namespace.

68
00:05:27,267 --> 00:05:29,686
And how does it actually work?

69
00:05:30,233 --> 00:05:33,636
Every cluster starts with a few namespaces:

70
00:05:33,636 --> 00:05:37,196
default (where things go when you name none),

71
00:05:37,196 --> 00:05:44,080
kube-system (the cluster's own components, lesson 02), kube-public and kube-node-lease.

72
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:50,251
A namespaced object belongs to one namespace; a cluster-scoped object (a Node,

73
00:05:50,251 --> 00:05:56,581
a Namespace itself, a StorageClass, a ClusterRole) belongs to the whole cluster.

74
00:05:56,581 --> 00:06:03,386
kubectl uses the namespace you give with -n, or the default namespace of your context.

75
00:06:03,386 --> 00:06:08,054
Namespaces separate names and permissions, not the network:

76
00:06:08,054 --> 00:06:11,852
by default a Pod in dev can reach a Pod in prod.

77
00:06:11,852 --> 00:06:15,808
NetworkPolicies (lesson 22) add network isolation.

